<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grand National Assembly of Turkey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Grand National Assembly</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grand National Assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> was opened by nationalist deputies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> on 23 April 1920, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in the name of the nation under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>. Its government won the War of Independence, abolished the Ottoman sultanate in 1922 and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> in 1923. It remains Turkey's parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grand-national-assembly-of-turkey.jpg" alt="Grand National Assembly of Turkey" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juris Tiltins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_Turkish_Parliament_(T%C3%BCrkiye_B%C3%BCy%C3%BCk_Millet_Meclisi).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> had parliamentary experience before 1920. The Ottoman First Constitutional Era lasted two years: the assembly was dissolved on 28 June 1877 after criticism of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a>, and the second assembly was dissolved by Sultan Abdul Hamid II on 14 February 1878, which suspended the Ottoman constitution of 1876. The Second Constitutional Era began on 23 July 1908 with the Young Turk Revolution. In both eras the General Assembly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> was bicameral, with a Senate chosen by the sultan and an elected Chamber of Deputies.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allied Powers</a> planned to dismember the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a>. In a speech of 19 March 1920 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> announced that an assembly with extraordinary powers would gather in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, and he said that members of the dispersed Ottoman Chamber of Deputies could also take part. Elections followed in the style of the earlier Chamber, and the new body held its first session on 23 April 1920. Until 1923 the provisional government was known as the Government of the Grand National Assembly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Multi-party politics began unsteadily. The Progressive Republican Party, founded in 1924 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>'s request, was closed after several months, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> of Ali Fethi Okyar, founded in 1930, was dissolved on 17 November 1930 after disorders. No further attempt was made until 1945. The Democrat Party, established the year after the National Development Party of 1945, won the general elections of 1950, and Celal Bayar became President and Adnan Menderes Prime Minister.</p><p>A military coup on 27 May 1960 arrested Menderes, Bayar, the ministers and the members of the Assembly and closed it. A Constituent Assembly drafted the 1961 Constitution, accepted in a referendum on 9 July 1961 by 61.17 percent of voters, which made parliament bicameral. After the coup of 12 September 1980 the 1982 Constitution, accepted by 91.37 percent on 7 November 1982, made it unicameral with 550 members and introduced a 10 percent electoral threshold. After the referendum of 2017 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> adopted a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presidential-democracy">presidential system</a>, and the number of deputies rose from 550 to 600.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The 600 deputies are elected for five years by the D'Hondt method of party-list proportional representation, from 87 electoral districts that represent the 81 provinces. Parties with at least 20 deputies may form a parliamentary group, and the work is divided among specialised committees. The Assembly has met in three buildings. The first was converted from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> headquarters of the Committee of Union and Progress, designed by Hasip Bey, and is now the War of Independence Museum. The second, by Vedat Tek, served from 1924 to 1960 and is now the Republic Museum. The present building in the Bakanlıklar neighbourhood of Ankara was designed by Clemens Holzmeister.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grand National Assembly of Turkey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Grand National Assembly</b> abolished the Sultanate on 1 November 1922, proclaimed the Republic of Turkey on 29 October 1923 and abolished the caliphate in March 1924.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Grand National Assembly</b> officially adopted it as the anthem on 12 March 1921, two and a half years before the Republic was proclaimed on 29 October 1923.</blockquote><a href="/songs/istiklal-marsi">Independence March</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Allies formally occupied Constantinople in March 1920, nationalist deputies and delegates met in Ankara and on 23 April 1920 opened the <b>Grand National Assembly</b>, which claimed sovereignty in the name of the nation.</blockquote><a href="/state/government-of-the-grand-national-assembly">Government of the Grand National Assembly</a></li></ul></section>
